# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 3 ways: augustine-4.logic.r2 has nothing from it; augustine-4.logic.r2.2 has "cw_created"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows; augustine-4.logic.r2.3 has "cw_first ∧ cw_created"
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r2.2 and augustine-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "a certain created wisdom was created before all things" is true and "this wisdom is created" is false. Is S1, "For although we find no time before it, for wisdom was created before all things; not that Wisdom which is altogether equal and coeternal unto Thee, our God, His Father, and by Whom all things were created, and in Whom, as the Beginning, Thou createdst heaven and earth; but that wisdom which is created, that is, the intellectual nature, which by contemplating the light, is light.", true then?
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r2 has "cw_created ∧ cw_called_wisdom"; augustine-4.logic.r2.2 and augustine-4.logic.r2.3 have "cw_called_wisdom"; they differ when only "this created intellectual nature is also called wisdom" is true, which the second allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "this created intellectual nature is also called wisdom" is true and "this wisdom is created" is false. Is S2, "For this, though created, is also called wisdom.", true then?
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 3 ways: augustine-4.logic.r2 has "cw_first → no_time_before" and "cw_created → began_creation ∧ creator_before" and "time_not_yet" and "time_not_yet → ¬began_time"; augustine-4.logic.r2.2 has "cw_first → no_time_before" and "time_not_yet" and "time_not_yet → ¬began_time" and "began_creation"; they differ when only "a certain created wisdom was created before all things", "we find no time before it", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before it" and "time itself was not yet" are true, which the first allows; augustine-4.logic.r2.3 has "cw_first → no_time_before" and "cw_created → began_creation" and "time_not_yet" and "time_not_yet → ¬began_time" and "began_creation → creator_before"
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "a certain created wisdom was created before all things", "we find no time before it", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before it" and "time itself was not yet" are true and "this wisdom is created", "it took from the Creator the beginning of its creation" and "it took the beginning of time" are false. Is S6, "Because this is also the Heaven of heavens for the Lord);--though we find no time before it (because that which hath been created before all things, precedeth also the creature of time), yet is the Eternity of the Creator Himself before it, from Whom, being created, it took the beginning, not indeed of time (for time itself was not yet), but of its creation.", true then?
augustine-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r2 and augustine-4.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; augustine-4.logic.r2.2 has "cw_created ∧ began_creation → creator_before"; they differ when only "this wisdom is created" and "it took from the Creator the beginning of its creation" are true, which the first allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: augustine-4.logic.r2 has "no_time_before ∧ creator_before ∧ began_creation ∧ ¬began_time"; augustine-4.logic.r2.2 and augustine-4.logic.r2.3 have "no_time_before ∧ creator_before"; they differ when only "we find no time before it", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before it", "it took from the Creator the beginning of its creation" and "it took the beginning of time" are true, which the second allows
augustine-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps augustine-4.logic.r2, to: Suppose "we find no time before it", "the Eternity of the Creator Himself is before it", "it took from the Creator the beginning of its creation" and "it took the beginning of time" are true. Is the conclusion, "Because this is also the Heaven of heavens for the Lord);--though we find no time before it (because that which hath been created before all things, precedeth also the creature of time), yet is the Eternity of the Creator Himself before it, from Whom, being created, it took the beginning, not indeed of time (for time itself was not yet), but of its creation.", true then?
